Gastric Cancer
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Persistent dyspepsia, Early satiety/Unintentional weight loss, Virchow's node, Ulcerated mass on endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-024
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting deep stomach pain, profound exhaustion, and an episode of vomiting dark material resembling coffee grounds.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult offspring.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a combination of severe, treatment-resistant epigastric pain, rapid significant weight loss, and an episode of hematemesis (coffee-ground emesis). This constellation strongly indicated an upper gastrointestinal bleed in the context of probable malignancy. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to evaluate haemodynamic stability, rule out catastrophic haemorrhage, and expedite direct hospital admission prior to standard consulting hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The gnawing pain in my stomach never stops, I've lost a massive amount of weight, and I just vomited up something that looked like black dirt."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 5-month history of chronic persistent dyspepsia and epigastric discomfort entirely unresponsive to standard high-dose acid suppression therapy. Reports profound early satiety, noting an inability to finish even small meals, leading to a profound, unexplained unintentional weight loss of 12kg. Describes a constant, deep, dull epigastric pain that is completely unrelieved by eating or antacids. Two hours ago, experienced an episode of coffee-ground emesis indicating upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Complains of severe fatigue and lethargy.
Functional Status: Bedbound, cachectic, and extremely frail. Unable to stand without assistance due to profound weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of haemodynamic collapse due to active bleeding; requires urgent escalation of care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears emaciated, pale, and chronically ill. Noticeable conjunctival pallor indicating marked anaemia.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is scaphoid. Deep palpation reveals a firm, tender, and irregular mass in the epigastric region. Careful examination of the left supraclavicular fossa reveals a firm, enlarged, and fixed lymph node (Virchow's node). A small, firm periumbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule) is also palpable. Digital rectal examination reveals black, tarry stool (melaena) on the glove. The presentation is classically defined by persistent dyspepsia, early satiety/unintentional weight loss, and Virchow's node; an ulcerated mass on endoscopy is definitively expected.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin containing small amounts of dark, coffee-ground material next to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with prescribed Esomeprazole 40mg BD without any symptomatic relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gastric Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Upper gastrointestinal bleeding and severe cachexia)
Differentials:

Benign Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of palpable metastatic disease (Virchow's node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule) and the profound, relentless weight loss despite maximal acid-suppression therapy.

Chronic Pancreatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of hematemesis and melaena, which point directly to a mucosal gastric source rather than isolated pancreatic exocrine failure.

Gastroparesis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of constant, deep pain, profound cachexia, and active bleeding, rather than isolated postprandial nausea and delayed emptying without structural disease.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Requires intensive medical intervention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic, end-stage 'red flag' signs of advanced gastric malignancy complicated by an acute upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage. The objective findings of resting tachycardia, hypotension, palpable metastatic nodes, and visible coffee-ground emesis mandate immediate high-level intervention. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the presence of active bleeding, position the patient safely, and coordinate emergency ambulance transfer for intravenous fluid resuscitation, blood transfusion, and urgent endoscopic/surgical evaluation. Deferring care would result in imminent hypovolaemic shock and potential mortality.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient to remain strictly nil by mouth. Initiated immediate contact with emergency services (000) for priority ambulance transport. Placed patient in a supine position to maximize cerebral perfusion.
Positive Behavioral Change: Do not attempt to sit up or walk to the bathroom while we wait for the ambulance. Keeping your body entirely flat mechanically ensures the limited blood volume you currently have is continuously directed to your brain and heart, preventing you from fainting and sustaining a severe head injury.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None currently; requires inpatient stabilization.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Emergency hospital admission required.

